<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/bungalow.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/bungalow-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/bungalow.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="684" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisal-services/">Services</a></li><li>Homeowner Claim Appraisal</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker kicker-l">Appraisal service</p><h1>Home Insurance Claim Appraiser for Arkansas Homeowners</h1><p class="lede">A homeowner claim rarely fails because nobody saw the damage. It fails on the details: a missed slope, a floor that can’t be matched, an interior trade nobody priced. We find those details and put a number on them.</p><div class="row"><a class="btn btn-red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="facts"><li><small>Property</small><b>Houses, condos, rentals</b></li><li><small>Perils</small><b>Storm, water, fire, ice</b></li><li><small>Pricing</small><b>Line-item, local</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap two"><article class="prose">
<p>Arkansas housing is a mix that estimating software doesn’t always handle well. Brick ranch homes from the 1960s and 70s fill much of Little Rock, Pine Bluff and Fort Smith. Northwest Arkansas adds thousands of newer subdivision homes with complex roof lines. Lake and river cabins, manufactured homes on permanent foundations and historic houses in neighborhoods like the Quapaw Quarter each bring their own pricing questions.</p>
<h2>Where homeowner estimates usually fall short</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roof scope.</strong> Partial-slope repairs approved where shingles can’t be matched or the manufacturer no longer makes the line.</li>
<li><strong>Interior continuity.</strong> One wall painted in a room with an open floor plan, or one room of flooring in a continuous run.</li>
<li><strong>Code and permit items.</strong> Drip edge, ice-and-water underlayment in valleys, ventilation and other items a local building department may require on replacement.</li>
<li><strong>Overhead and profit.</strong> Left off claims that clearly need a general contractor to coordinate several trades.</li>
<li><strong>Contents and additional living expense.</strong> Undervalued or ignored after water and fire losses.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How a homeowner appraisal runs</h2>
<p>We start with a review of your declarations page, the carrier’s full estimate and your photos. If the gap is large enough to justify appraisal, we inspect, build our own estimate and, once appraisal is demanded, serve as your named appraiser. If the gap is small, we will say so and suggest a documented supplement request instead.</p>
<h2>Owners we help</h2>
<p>Owner-occupants, landlords with a few rental houses, condo unit owners with an HO-6 policy, and families whose home is being rebuilt after a total loss. We work across all 75 Arkansas counties.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do you work for homeowners with small claims?<svg class="chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>We review any disputed claim. For gaps under a few thousand dollars, a supplement with good photos is usually cheaper than appraisal, and we’ll tell you that.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will my insurer cancel me for invoking appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal is a right written into your policy. Questions about renewals or nonrenewal rules should go to the Arkansas Insurance Department or an attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you help after my contractor and adjuster disagree?<svg class="chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. That disagreement is exactly what appraisal is designed to resolve, provided coverage itself isn’t in dispute.</p></div></details></div>
